.include "8clocks.jsim"
.include "nominal.jsim"
.include "stdcell.jsim"

*******************************************************************************
* Full adder and 32-bit Ripple-Carry Adder
*******************************************************************************
.subckt fa a b c_in sum c_out
X1 a b n1 nand2
X2 a n1 n2 nand2
X3 b n1 n3 nand2
X4 n2 n3 n4 nand2

X5 n4 c_in n5 nand2
X6 n4 n5 n6 nand2
X7 c_in n5 n7 nand2
X8 n6 n7 sum nand2

X9 n1 n5 c_out nand2
.ends

.subckt add32 a[31:0] b[31:0] cin0 sum[31:0] cout[31:30]
.connect cin0 carry0

Xfa a[31:0] b[31:0] carry[31:0] sum[31:0] carry[32:1] fa

.connect carry32 cout31
.connect carry31 cout30
.ends

*******************************************************************************
* Counter 32 Subcircuit
*******************************************************************************
.subckt counter32 reset a[31:0] f[31:0] clk
* Add the increment to the current register value, with no carry-in.
Xadd a[31:0] f[31:0] 0 sum[31:0] carry[31:30] add32

* A high reset selects zero for all 32 register inputs.
Xmux reset#32 sum[31:0] 0#32 next[31:0] mux2

* Store the selected value on each rising clock edge and feed it back.
Xreg next[31:0] clk#32 f[31:0] dreg

.ends

*******************************************************************************
* The current behavior of the 32-bit register can be explaained as the following:
*       - During the incremenent, it goes through the adder and adds the waveform values specirfied.
*
*       - During the decrement, as the system doesn't technically know when a number is negative or positive, justs adds it anyways.
*        In the cases of decrement, the adder simply adds the two's complement eq2uivalent of a value to the current register.
*
*       - In RESET situations, the MUX utilizes the reset over the sum in the MUX and will overide the "sum" computed by add32 if active.
*       In a sense, it basically RESET forces the output to zero even if Add32 calculated something.
* 
* In regards to visibly significant delays, it takes time for the ripple counter to calculate the value through every bit of the 32-bit adder.
* Coupled with the fact it also settles the mux, the register does not get the final computed value that may have visible delays in faster clocks.
* The "green bar of death" is basically us watching the T_pd of the clock cycles and the "bar" being the system being in an unsettled state.
* Visually representing something mid-computation, the system does not want to output results until it has been fully settled.
*******************************************************************************

Xcounter32 reset incr[31:0] reg[31:0] clk1 counter32

*******************************************************************************
* Faster-clock stress test: 2ns period (500MHz), versus clk1's 10ns (100MHz).
* Reuse the same reset and increment waveforms with a separate counter.
*******************************************************************************
Vfastclk fastclk 0 pulse(0, 5, 0.9ns, 0.1ns, 0.1ns, 0.9ns, 2ns)
Xfastcounter reset incr[31:0] fastreg[31:0] fastclk counter32

*******************************************************************************
* A faster running clock, while possible, does little to benefit the current situation. 
* The ripple-carry would still need to go through all 32 stages, still taking a fixed amount of time.
* Checking in mid-calculation gives the green bars of death without the actual usable values.
* Beyond that, the ripple process would be more frequently disrupted and as such, could disrupt counting anything meaningful.
* Ideally, the clock period should be atleast as long as the settling time of the add32 and mux2, following the fixed time of the hardware.
*******************************************************************************

*******************************************************************************
* This "reset" waveform sets up a reset signal that has a delayed start
* and then goes to the next value every 50ns.
* The values "1 0 0 0 1 0" indicate that the reset signal only turns on twice.
* (But at which times?)
*******************************************************************************
Wreset reset nrz(0, 5, 50ns, 14.8ns, 0.001ns, 0.001ns)
+ 1 0 0 0 1 0

*******************************************************************************
* This "incr" waveform sets up a increment value for your counter32 circuit.
* Note that it does NOT have a delayed start. The values "0 1 3 -1" roughly
* translate to the following "commands" in Java-like syntax:
*   reg += 0;  //  0 means don't touch the value
*   reg++;     //  1 means increment by 1
*   reg += 3;  //  3 means increment by 3
*   reg--;     // -1 means decrement by 1
* But note that these "commands" will happen EVERY positive clock edge (and
* only when your reset signal is off)!
*******************************************************************************
Wincr incr[31:0] nrz(0, 5, 50ns, 0ns, 0.001ns, 0.001ns)
+ 0 1 3 -1

*******************************************************************************
* Plot our reset waveform, our incr waveform, clk1, and your 32-bit register.
* Note: sd() displays a range of nodes as a signed decimal (instead of hex)
*******************************************************************************
.tran 300ns
.plot reset
.plot sd(incr[31:0])
.plot clk[8:1]
.plot sd(reg[31:0])
.plot fastclk
.plot sd(fastreg[31:0])